<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Langston Hughes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1901">1901</a>–<a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as James Mercer Langston Hughes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Langston Hughes was an American poet, social activist, novelist, playwright and columnist from Joplin, Missouri, an early innovator of jazz poetry and a leader of the Harlem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. His poem The Negro Speaks of Rivers appeared in The Crisis in 1921, and his first collection, The Weary Blues, in 1926. He also wrote plays, short stories, novels and nonfiction, and from 1943 published stories about Jesse B. Semple, an everyday black man in Harlem. From 1942 to 1962, as the civil rights movement grew, he wrote a weekly column for The Chicago Defender. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City in 1967 after surgery related to prostate cancer.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/langston-hughes.jpg" alt="Langston Hughes" width="330" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Van Vechten · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Langston_Hughes_by_Carl_Van_Vechten_Trim.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shuffle Along, a musical of 1921 produced, composed, directed and written by African Americans, ran for 504 performances on Broadway; <b>Langston Hughes</b> later wrote that Manhattan’s Black Renaissance “began with Shuffle Along”.</blockquote><a href="/movement/harlem-renaissance">Harlem Renaissance · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Harlem Renaissance of the 1920s and 1930s, whose writers, among them <b>Langston Hughes</b>, Claude McKay, Alain Locke and W.E.B. Du Bois, addressed race relations and double consciousness, was a further influence.</blockquote><a href="/movement/negritude">Négritude · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The Golden Gate Quartet recorded it in 1957, Louis Armstrong for the album Louis and the Good Book in 1958, and the Robert Shaw Chorale in 1964, with liner notes by <b>Langston Hughes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/go-down-moses">Go Down Moses</a></li></ul></section>
